Ring x support: stop allocating the half of U3 that is identically zero.

Patch 0010 reported that F-barES-FEM-T4 at c = 1 could not be built on the
LMESH_m0p0120 cell: mastruct.c pushes one entry per off-diagonal upper-
triangle (dof,dof) pair of every element onto mast1 with NO deduplication,
the index is a 32-bit ITG, and a U3 element dense over its 39-node stencil
needed 5.16e9 insertions -- past 2^31, so ccx died in u_realloc with a
negative allocation size.

THE ELEMENT WAS NEVER DENSE OVER ITS STENCIL.

K_vol = (K V_h) tbar^T sbar is rank one and its two factors have DIFFERENT
supports.  tbar is the UNSMOOTHED edge divergence of eq. (1) -- u3vol builds
it only from the tets that contain the edge (the elcur list) -- so its
support is exactly the U2 edge ring, 6.5 nodes on this cell.  Only sbar
carries the wide E A^c support.  So s(ii,jj) can be nonzero only for i in
the RING and j in the SUPPORT, and the

    (support - ring) x (support - ring)

block that mastruct was allocating is identically zero.  Dropping it is not
an approximation and not a tolerance: those entries cannot be written.

  fbares.py      writes the U3 connectivity RING FIRST -- [edge nodes, rest
                 of ring, rest of support] -- and encodes nring in the type
                 name as U3<r><xy>, r = LETTERS[nring-1].  elements.f keys
                 the *USER ELEMENT lookup on label(2:5), so 'U' plus four
                 characters is all ccx can carry and three suffix letters is
                 the most that fits.  Ring sizes past 26 are refused with
                 that reason named.
  mastruct.c     decodes nring from lakon(3:3) and runs the insertion loop
                 to ndof*nring instead of ndof*nope.  Restricting the OUTER
                 loop alone is what makes this correct, and only because the
                 ring is first: the inner loop starts at jj, so every
                 unordered pair {ring, support} is still generated once.
  mafillsmas.f   the same decode, row loop to 3*nring.  The column loop
                 still spans the whole support -- a ring row couples to
                 every support column, and mastruct allocated exactly that.
  e_c3d_u3.f     row loop to nring, plus the guard below.

THE ORDERING IS NOT TAKEN ON TRUST.

A deck whose ring is not first, or whose label disagrees with it, would have
real tbar rows dropped SILENTLY -- and no patch test can see that, because a
uniform field gives the missing rows nothing to carry.  Worse, a deck from
the PREVIOUS generator is named U3<xy>, so lakon(3:3) decodes as nring = 1
and mastruct and the element agree on the same wrong answer: no error, just
a different element.

e_c3d_u3 therefore checks that tbar vanishes past nring and stops with the
element number, its nring, and the offending node.  Verified: the old
ptf1.inp through the new binary stops with

  *ERROR in e_c3d_u3: element 3151
         lakon(3:3) says the ring is the first 1 nodes, but tbar is
         nonzero at position 2 node 7

tests/verify_fbares_deck.py checks the same thing in the deck, where the
element can be named before a two-hour solve rather than after one.

WHAT IT BUYS

Insertions fall by a factor ~3.3 at c = 1:

  cell            c   before     after     outcome
  LMESH_m0p0240   0   7.23e7     2.7e7     -
  LMESH_m0p0240   1   1.12e9     3.4e8     30.05M -> ~ nnz falls with it
  LMESH_m0p0120   1   5.16e9     1.5e9     BUILDS: 600662 eq, 68.6M nnz

VERIFICATION -- NOTHING MOVED

  * LMESH_m0p0240 c=1, the production cell: E_x = 6153500000.0, BIT-IDENTICAL
    to the dense-structure run of patch 0003.  R is still 2.4448.
  * patch test, general six-component strain on a distorted mesh, c = 0, 1, 2:
    max|u - eps.X|/max|u| = 3.165e-07 for every arm, the same value the C3D4
    control and the dense structure gave.
  * confined compression, c = 0, 1, 2: C1111 = 2.204400e+08, exact.

The entries dropped are zero, so any change in these numbers would have meant
the ring ordering or the nring decode was wrong.  None moved.

WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX

LMESH_m0p0120 at c = 1 now builds its structure and dies in the FACTORISATION
instead: 600662 equations at 68.6M nonzeros through the unsymmetric PARDISO
path asks for 45 GB of virtual memory and was OOM-killed twice at ~22-24 GB
anon-rss on this 30 GB machine.  That is a different wall and this patch does
not claim it.  Note the two kills were GLOBAL, not cgroup -- neither run was
under ccx_capped, which is what that wrapper exists to prevent; and swap here
is zram (RAM-backed) so it cannot help, while /tmp is tmpfs so a 263 MB deck
staged there is itself RAM.

